---

<Base
  dateModified={sourceGapCheckDate}
  title="How to Use Meta Muse: Step-by-Step Setup Guide"
  description="Set up Meta Muse at muse.ai or in the standalone app. Compare Free, Power and Maximum, add Connectors, and review permissions and approve sensitive actions."
  canonicalPath="/how-to-use-meta-muse/"
  pageType="article"
  breadcrumbs={[
    { label: 'Home', href: '/' },
    { label: 'How to Use Meta Muse' },
  ]}
>
  <article class="content">
    <header class="hero-panel">
    <p class="eyebrow">Setup guide</p>
    <h1>How to Use Meta Muse</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Use Muse on the web, mobile, Mac, or through WhatsApp where available.</p>
    <StatusChips checked={sourceGapCheckDate} />
    </header>
    <p class="hero-context">This guide covers
      cloud tasks, local Mac access, Connectors, and the permissions to review before you begin.</p>

    <section aria-labelledby="requirements-heading">
      <h2 id="requirements-heading">What You Need Before You Start</h2>
      <p>
        Setup is built around a Meta account and a way to reach the product. Meta Muse has its own
        iOS, Android, and Mac apps and a web version, separate from Meta AI. WhatsApp is another
        way to chat with Muse where supported.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>A Meta account for signing in.</li>
        <li>Access to muse.ai, an iOS or Android phone, or a supported Mac.</li>
        <li>A Google account if you want to connect Gmail or Google Calendar.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Muse for Small Business is available in the US and Canada for people 18 and over. Personal
        Muse <a href={updateSources.japan}>launched in Japan on October 23, 2026</a> on iOS, Android, and muse.ai.
        Meta’s announcement described Japan as the first region outside North America.
        It includes Gmail, Google Calendar, and Instagram among services Japanese users can connect,
        and names Stripe, Shopify, Trip.com, Agoda, and Klook as global integrations for shopping and travel.
        The announcement says Meta is pursuing local partnerships with Mercari, Money Forward ME, and Ticket Pia.
        The same post said Japan availability for voice, Realtime Avatar (labeled English-only), and Muse’s own email address would be announced separately.
        A November 20, 2026 check did not find a later official Japan announcement of those features.
        Personal Muse has also launched in Singapore, <a href={updateSources.mexico}>Mexico</a>, and
        <a href={updateSources.australia}>Australia</a>. In Mexico
        it is available in Spanish on iOS, Android, the web, and WhatsApp. In Australia, Meta says it
        is available on iOS, Android, muse.ai, and the Mac app, with AI glasses listed as coming soon.
        Subscriptions and
        connectors may vary by region and account. Check access at <a href="https://muse.ai/">muse.ai</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="steps-heading">
      <h2 id="steps-heading">Five Setup Steps</h2>
      <p>
        The setup sequence starts with access and ends with a clear understanding of where tasks
        run. Add other services as needed, but first review Facebook, Instagram, and Threads:
        they connect automatically when accounts share an Accounts Center. Review Mac permissions separately.
      </p>

      <ol class="page-steps">
        <li>
          <h3>Install the App and Sign In</h3>
          <p>
            Open <a href="https://muse.ai/">muse.ai</a> or get the mobile or Mac app from the
            <a href="https://ai.meta.com/muse/download/">official Muse download page</a>. Sign
            in with your Meta account after the app or page loads.
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li>Install the app or open the web product.</li>
            <li>Sign in with your existing Meta account.</li>
            <li>Complete any account prompts shown during the first session.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            Meta Muse is not the same product as Meta AI. Confirm that you are opening the correct
            app or web address before you continue.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <h3>Decide Between the Free Tier and a Paid Plan</h3>
          <p>
            Meta Muse has limited free weekly usage, Power ($20/month, 500M Muse tokens/week),
            and Maximum ($100/month, 3B/week). If you reach a limit, additional tokens are available;
            free users can also upgrade or wait for the limit to refresh. Start with the free tier when you are still deciding whether the agent can
            complete the kinds of tasks you have in mind.
          </p>
          <p>
            Compare the <a href="/meta-muse-pricing/#plans-heading">official plan and weekly-token table</a>
            before choosing Power or Maximum. Benefits may vary by region and account.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <h3>Review Connector Connections</h3>
          <p>
            Gmail and Google Calendar require a connection; add them when needed. Facebook, Instagram,
            and Threads connect automatically for accounts in the same Accounts Center.
            Review the requested scope; a connection does not approve every action.
            You can change permissions or disconnect a service later.
          </p>
          <p>
            Review and approve sensitive actions before they happen. Under Meta’s Connector guidelines,
            sensitive writes require approval each time; other tool permissions can depend on your settings.
            Grant only the access needed for the task. Our
            <a href="/meta-muse-review/">Meta Muse review</a> explains why privacy is the main
            concern raised in current reporting.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <h3>Understand Where It Runs</h3>
          <p>
            Cloud browsing runs in Muse Secure VM, a separate virtual machine with enough
            storage, CPU, and memory for real work. It includes its own browser, and the environment
            is isolated from your local computer.
          </p>
          <p>
            The Mac app adds local access when authorized. Follow the
            <a href="#mac-heading">Mac permission checklist below</a>; review both local access
            and connected-service permissions.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <h3>Try a Small Set of First Tasks</h3>
          <p>
            Begin with tasks that have a clear result and a limited scope. Give Meta Muse enough
            detail to understand the target, any constraints, and what a finished task should look
            like.
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <strong>Research a webpage:</strong> Ask it to open a specific page and collect the
              details you need, then check the result against the source.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Fill out a form:</strong> Supply the exact information for each field and ask
              it to complete the form on a specified website.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Place a food order:</strong> Point it to a restaurant page with the items,
              quantities, and order details you want, then review and approve the purchase before it is placed.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Send an email:</strong> Use a connected mailbox or, if eligible, Muse’s own
              address. Give Muse the recipient, subject, and message requirements. Review the draft
              and any approval prompt before sending.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Align upcoming commitments:</strong> Ask it to check calendar events and
              relevant email, then create a message that summarizes the schedule.
            </li>
          </ol>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="new-entry-points-heading">
      <h2 id="new-entry-points-heading">Muse Mail, Spaces, watches, browsing, calling, and Try On</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/muse-email/">Muse Mail</a>: Paid subscribers in the US and Canada can ask Muse
          to set up a dedicated @muse.ai address, or open the mail tab from their avatar. Official Help
          says Muse asks for approval before sending.</li>
        <li><a href="/muse-spaces/">Spaces</a>: Tell Muse what you want to build. You can put the
          resulting space in the sidebar or main tabs, hide it, or schedule when it appears. A November 18
          Newsroom post also says Canadians can now build spaces.</li>
        <li><a href="/muse-apple-watch-wear-os/">Apple Watch and Wear OS</a>: Meta announced both
          platforms. Official @Muse later said voice is available on Apple Watch and Wear OS. Check the official App Store listing for Apple Watch compatibility.</li>
        <li><a href="/muse-voice-avatar/">Voice</a>: Meta began the rollout in the United States, later described real-time voice in Australia, and a November 18 Newsroom post says Voice is rolling out in Canada on mobile, web, and Mac. The October 23 Japan post left Japan voice availability for a later announcement.</li>
        <li><a href="/muse-chrome-extension/">Muse Chrome Extension</a>: Official Help says you can
          ask Muse in Chat to download it. Muse then browses in your personal Chrome, in a separate tab group,
          which is distinct from the in-chat browser and from Muse for Mac. The Chrome Web Store listing now shows version 2.1.3, updated November 18, 2026, without new feature notes.</li>
        <li><a href="/muse-outbound-calling/">Outbound calling</a>: Official @Muse said this is available
          for paid subscribers in the United States, a narrower scope than Muse Mail. Official Help now lists 10 phone calls per day on Power and 20 on Maximum; that does not, by itself, widen eligibility.</li>
        <li><a href="/muse-try-on/">Try On</a>: Official @Muse said Muse will prompt you when you’re shopping
          to show how clothes might look. Official sources reviewed here do not specify where it is available.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="mac-heading">
      <h2 id="mac-heading">Muse for Mac: Local Files and App Permissions</h2>
      <p>Muse for Mac can work with local files and apps including Messages (iMessage), Notes,
        Reminders, Mail, and Calendar. This access is separate from the cloud Secure VM.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>macOS permissions:</strong> Full Disk Access covers files; Automation permits app actions; Notifications provides task updates.</li>
        <li><strong>Per-app choices:</strong> Off, Read only, or Read and interact.</li>
        <li><strong>Revoke access:</strong> Review File System Access in Muse Settings and permissions in macOS System Settings → Privacy &amp; Security.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Review important actions before confirming. See <a href="https://www.meta.com/help/superintelligence/1126304576638594/">Meta’s Mac access guide</a>.</p>
    </section>
    <section aria-labelledby="connectors-heading">
      <h2 id="connectors-heading">Personal, Business, and Device Connectors</h2>
      <p><a href="/muse-for-small-business/">Muse for Small Business</a> adds connections to Asana,
        Box, Canva, Dropbox, Figma, Granola, HighLevel, Intuit QuickBooks, Klaviyo, Lovable,
        Notion, Shopify, Slack, Stripe, and Zoom. Meta also lists Facebook Pages, Instagram
        professional account analytics, and Meta ad accounts. These business connections are
        distinct from the automatic personal-account connections described below.</p>
      <p>Check Settings → Connectors for your account’s current list. Developers and partners can
        learn about building integrations at the <a href="https://platform.muse.ai/">Muse Connector Platform</a>.
        Source: <a href="https://about.fb.com/news/2026/09/introducing-muse-small-business/">Meta’s small-business announcement</a>.</p>
      <p>On November 13, 2026, Muse published the <a href="/personal-agent-protocol/">Personal Agent Protocol (Poppy)</a> version 1.0.
        A business can publish poppy.json and offer a website, APIs, an MCP server, or its own agent.
        Early examples in that post, including BYLT coming soon, Mercury through Sierra, and a Hertz pilot with Decagon,
        are not the Small Business connector list above. Protocol participation does not change the US and Canada eligibility for Muse for Small Business.</p>
      <p>Facebook, Instagram, and Threads connect automatically when accounts share an Accounts Center.
        For other services, use Settings → Connectors or ask Muse to connect one.</p>
      <p>For personal travel planning, Meta <a href={updateSources.expedia}>announced Expedia as
        a Muse Connector</a> on November 10. The announcement does not specify which booking,
        payment, or refund actions it supports.</p>
      <p>On November 18, Meta <a href={updateSources.loblaw}>announced Loblaw as the first Canadian
        retailer to connect to Muse</a>. Starting that day, Canadian shoppers can use Muse to help
        with meal planning, build a basket, and check out with participating Loblaw banners. The
        announcement does not name those banners. Meta says Muse can remember brand preferences and
        information such as food allergies. As with other purchases, Meta says Muse checks with you
        before sensitive actions such as making a purchase.</p>
      <p>Meta’s <a href={updateSources.australia}>November 17 Australia launch post</a> says Canva
        is also available as a Connector for Muse users around the world, not only as a Small Business
        Connector. The same post lists early local Australian partners that will roll out progressively,
        with some experiences becoming available after launch: Canva, Domain, Kogan, MECCA, Officeworks,
        realestate.com.au, SWEAT, ShopBack, and Ticketek. It also mentions services people in Australia
        can already connect, such as Gmail, Google Calendar, Instagram, and OpenTable.</p>
      <p>Custom Connectors can use service API credentials; Meta does not review them. Apple Health
        and Android SMS access is managed in device settings after setup. Disconnecting a Connector
        stops further exchange but may leave earlier information in Muse’s history or memories.</p>
      <p>Source: <a href="https://www.meta.com/help/superintelligence/1687253048996149/">Meta’s Connector guide</a>.</p>
    </section>
    <section aria-labelledby="chats-artifacts-heading">
      <h2 id="chats-artifacts-heading">Side Chats, Artifacts, and Library</h2>
      <p>Use side chats to keep a project or topic organized outside the main conversation.
        In the Chat tab, open the top-left menu and select the pencil-and-notepad icon to start
        a side chat. Muse stays aware across both the main chat and side chats.</p>
      <p>Upload a file or ask Muse to create one. Artifacts can include PDFs, spreadsheets,
        documents, images, and podcasts. Completed Artifacts are saved to a workspace in the
        Library tab and shared in the thread; open Library to find the finished result.</p>
      <p>Source: <a href="https://www.meta.com/ja-jp/help/superintelligence/1331373868832401/">Meta Help: how to get started with Muse (Japanese edition)</a>.</p>
    </section>
    <section aria-labelledby="whatsapp-heading">
      <h2 id="whatsapp-heading">Chat with Muse in WhatsApp</h2>
      <p>Meta also lists WhatsApp as a way to chat with Muse. Check the available setup in your account;
        this is an entry point to the personal agent, not a Muse Code subscription.</p>
      <p>Source: <a href="https://ai.meta.com/muse/">Meta’s Muse product page</a>.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="limits-heading">
      <h2 id="limits-heading">Practical Limits and Permissions</h2>
      <p>
        The product's operating model sets several practical boundaries. These limits shape which
        tasks fit cloud browsing or authorized local Mac access.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>The cloud browser is separate from your device.</strong> Local files and apps require the Mac client and the access you authorize.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Connected tasks need permission.</strong> Connectors such as Gmail and Google Calendar
          require account access. Browser tasks also depend on each website’s requirements.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>It is not a replacement for review.</strong> A completed action still needs to
          be checked, especially when a purchase, email, or form submission has consequences.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Availability varies.</strong> Business eligibility and personal-agent launches differ;
          confirm region, age requirements, and subscription access in your account.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <!-- TODO: verify supported task limits, exclusions, and platform restrictions. -->
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="troubleshooting-heading">
      <h2 id="troubleshooting-heading">Troubleshooting</h2>
      <p>
        The questions below cover the setup points that affect the largest number of first-time
        users: account access, payment, connected services, task limits, and the execution
        environment.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="sources-heading">
      <h2 id="sources-heading">Official Sources</h2>
      <p>Business features, availability, and getting-started guidance checked <time datetime="2026-10-30">2026-10-30</time>; earlier capability sources checked October 29. Japan availability and the Personal Agent Protocol checked <time datetime={sourceGapCheckDate}>November 20, 2026</time>.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><a href={updateSources.japan}>Meta: personal Muse launch in Japan, October 23</a></li>
        <li><a href={updateSources.poppy}>Muse: Personal Agent Protocol (Poppy) 1.0, November 13</a></li>
        <li><a href={updateSources.loblaw}>Meta: Loblaw as first Canadian retail partner, November 18</a></li>
        <li><a href={updateSources.tryOn}>Official @Muse: introducing Try On</a></li>
        <li><a href={updateSources.australia}>Meta: personal Muse launch in Australia, November 17</a></li>
        <li><a href={updateSources.chromeHelp}>Meta Help: How your Muse agent browses the web</a></li>
        <li><a href={updateSources.calling}>Muse: outbound calling for paid US subscribers</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://muse.ai/business">Muse for Small Business: eligibility and connectors</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://about.fb.com/news/2026/10/muse-is-now-available-in-singapore-the-worlds-first-personal-ai-agent-built-for-everyone/">Meta: personal Muse launch in Singapore</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.meta.com/help/subscriptions/1021145227643680/">Meta Help: subscriptions and availability</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://about.fb.com/news/2026/09/introducing-muse-personal-ai-agent/">Meta: launch, cloud VM, privacy, and training controls</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://ai.meta.com/muse/download/">Muse for Mac and mobile downloads</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.meta.com/help/superintelligence/1126304576638594/">Mac files and app access</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.meta.com/help/superintelligence/1687253048996149/">Connectors and automatic Meta connections</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.meta.com/help/superintelligence/1047255454427887/">Privacy, safety, and security</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://platform.muse.ai/docs">Meta: Connector permissions and sensitive-action approval</a></li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <FAQ items={faqItems} />

    <RelatedLinks links={relatedLinks} />
  </article>
</Base>
